'''Ancient Southeastern Kaftia''' refers to the period from approximately 4000 BCE to 500 BCE, beginning with the emergence of the first literate civilizations in the region and ending with the expansion of the [[Mesogean Empire]] into Kaftia. This period witnessed the rise and fall of multiple civilizations, the development of writing, the formation of the [[Kingdom of Tzur]] and the emergence of [[Judaism]], and the expansion of the [[Amara Empire]].

The ancient period represents the transition from the prehistoric cultural traditions documented in [[Prehistoric Southeastern Kaftia]] to complex, literate, state-level societies. The [[Qush civilization]], emerging around 4000 BCE in the river valleys of [[Jarin]], developed the first writing system on [[Adonia]]. The [[Kingdom of Tzur]], established at the strategic [[Triphylian Isthmus]], became the birthplace of Judaism. The [[Amara Empire]] dominated the western regions following its conquest of Qush. The period ended with the beginning of Mesogean expansion into Kaftia, which would transform the region during the subsequent [[Classical Southeastern Kaftia|Classical period]].

== Overview ==

The ancient period in Southeastern Kaftia saw the emergence of seven major civilizational centers, each developing distinct political structures, economies, and cultural traditions:

